                },
                {
                    "author": {
                        "avatarUrl": "https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/24854248?u=d09fd974c6720eba3039db6beb2f1c1efecdb51d&v=4",
                        "login": "echasnovski",
                        "resourcePath": "/echasnovski",
                        "url": "https://github.com/echasnovski"
                    },
                    "authorAssociation": "MEMBER",
                    "body": "> Basically, I want the same behavior as other editors such as VS Code.\r\n\r\nThis is Neovim which itself has a long history (including Vim) of how it handles commenting and 'mini.comment' represents exactly that.\r\n\r\nAs far as I can tell with the brief VS Code tests, 'mini.comment' behaves more like `<C-/>` (i.e. per-line toggle comment which stops selection) and not like `<C-S-a>` (i.e. toggle block comment which preserves selection).\r\n\r\nThe closest to VS Code behavior of `<C-S-a>` can be achieved with the solution above, but probably also `and context.action == 'comment'` condition needs to be removed (to preserve selection regardless of the action performed). This will work only on per line basis as ['mini.comment' doesn't support block comments](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/919b953f044d97a2243cc2d1f0b316a199434127/doc/mini-comment.txt#L21).\r\n\r\nMy sincere suggestion would be to get used to the mini.comment' behavior as most Neovim (and Vim) operators don't preserve visual selection after operating on it.",
                    "bodyHTML": "<blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Basically, I want the same behavior as other editors such as VS Code.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">This is Neovim which itself has a long history (including Vim) of how it handles commenting and 'mini.comment' represents exactly that.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">As far as I can tell with the brief VS Code tests, 'mini.comment' behaves more like <code class=\"notranslate\">&lt;C-/&gt;</code> (i.e. per-line toggle comment which stops selection) and not like <code class=\"notranslate\">&lt;C-S-a&gt;</code> (i.e. toggle block comment which preserves selection).</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">The closest to VS Code behavior of <code class=\"notranslate\">&lt;C-S-a&gt;</code> can be achieved with the solution above, but probably also <code class=\"notranslate\">and context.action == 'comment'</code> condition needs to be removed (to preserve selection regardless of the action performed). This will work only on per line basis as <a href=\"https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/919b953f044d97a2243cc2d1f0b316a199434127/doc/mini-comment.txt#L21\">'mini.comment' doesn't support block comments</a>.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">My sincere suggestion would be to get used to the mini.comment' behavior as most Neovim (and Vim) operators don't preserve visual selection after operating on it.</p>",
                    "bodyText": "Basically, I want the same behavior as other editors such as VS Code.\n\nThis is Neovim which itself has a long history (including Vim) of how it handles commenting and 'mini.comment' represents exactly that.\nAs far as I can tell with the brief VS Code tests, 'mini.comment' behaves more like <C-/> (i.e. per-line toggle comment which stops selection) and not like <C-S-a> (i.e. toggle block comment which preserves selection).\nThe closest to VS Code behavior of <C-S-a> can be achieved with the solution above, but probably also and context.action == 'comment' condition needs to be removed (to preserve selection regardless of the action performed). This will work only on per line basis as 'mini.comment' doesn't support block comments.\nMy sincere suggestion would be to get used to the mini.comment' behavior as most Neovim (and Vim) operators don't preserve visual selection after operating on it.",
